prompt:
These are corporate environmental claims that our carbon-accounting rule set did not flag.
For each, identify any technical term, metric, or accounting concept that could hide a scoping or
boundary problem but is not covered by these nine existing rules: market-based vs location-based
Scope 2, REC/PPA grid matching, carbon neutral offsets, missing baseline year, missing scope
boundary, cherry-picked PUE, water accounting, future promises without milestones, matching
language.

CLAIMS:
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_312", "text": "Amazon's global PUE of 1.14 is nearly 9% better than the public cloud industry average of 1.25."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_313", "text": "Amazon's global PUE of 1.14 is 30% better than the on-premises enterprise data center average of 1.63."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_332", "text": "Cooling water use was reduced globally by 938 million liters in 2025."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_333", "text": "Amazon's global data center Water Use Effectiveness (WUE) was reduced to 0.12 liters per kWh in 2025, a 20% improvement from 2024."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_334", "text": "Amazon's global data center WUE of 0.12 liters per kWh in 2025 represents a 52% improvement from 2021."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_346", "text": "Select Kindle Scribe models featured 100% recycled cobalt in batteries."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_347", "text": "Select Kindle Scribe models featured 85% recycled tin in the solder used on printed circuit boards."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_348", "text": "Amazon is piloting air-cooled heat sinks with 50% recycled aluminum."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_350", "text": "In 2025, Amazon reduced waste per shipped unit by 5% compared to 2024."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_351", "text": "In 2025, Amazon diverted 84% of its waste from landfill."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_352", "text": "In 2025, 82% of Amazon's waste was diverted through recycling or composting."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_367", "text": "34% of all products sold at Whole Foods Market were organic in 2025."}
{"claim_id": "amzn_sr_368", "text": "61% of fresh produce sold at Whole Foods Market were organic in 2025."}

Output ONLY a JSON array: [{"term": "...", "why_it_matters": "one sentence",
"example_claim_id": "..."}]. Return [] if nothing new.

text:
[
  {
    "term": "industry average benchmark source and vintage",
    "why_it_matters": "Comparing PUE against an 'industry average' or 'enterprise average' is only meaningful if the benchmark dataset, its collection methodology, geographic scope, and publication year are disclosed; an undisclosed or outdated benchmark can make any figure look favorable.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_312"
  },
  {
    "term": "like-for-like workload normalization in PUE comparisons",
    "why_it_matters": "Comparing a hyperscale cloud PUE to on-premises enterprise data centers conflates facilities running at very different IT load densities and utilization rates, making the efficiency gap appear larger than an apples-to-apples comparison would show.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_313"
  },
  {
    "term": "absolute vs. intensity metric reporting (water)",
    "why_it_matters": "Reporting a reduction in absolute water volume (938 million liters) alongside a WUE intensity ratio obscures whether total consumption rose or fell as the fleet expanded; an intensity improvement can coexist with higher absolute consumption.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_332"
  },
  {
    "term": "WUE boundary definition (tower vs. campus vs. grid water)",
    "why_it_matters": "WUE can be scoped to only on-site evaporative cooling towers, excluding water consumed upstream by the electric grid or in off-site district cooling plants, silently understating true water impact.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_333"
  },
  {
    "term": "multi-year intensity baseline consistency",
    "why_it_matters": "A 52% WUE improvement since 2021 is unverifiable if the 2021 baseline methodology, fleet composition, or metering coverage differed from 2025, allowing favorable restating of the starting point.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_334"
  },
  {
    "term": "'select models' or partial-portfolio scoping for material claims",
    "why_it_matters": "Attributing recycled-content achievements only to 'select' SKUs without disclosing what share of total unit volume or material mass they represent lets a small subset carry an outsized impression of fleet-wide progress.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_346"
  },
  {
    "term": "recycled-content mass fraction vs. component-level fraction",
    "why_it_matters": "Stating '85% recycled tin in solder' refers to a minor-mass component; without disclosing solder's share of total PCB or product mass, the figure conveys little about overall recycled-material intensity.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_347"
  },
  {
    "term": "pilot vs. deployed-at-scale scope disclosure",
    "why_it_matters": "Describing a recycled-aluminum heat sink as a 'pilot' without stating deployment volume or timeline creates an impression of progress that may represent a negligible fraction of actual production.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_348"
  },
  {
    "term": "waste-per-unit denominator definition",
    "why_it_matters": "'Shipped unit' can be defined as a package, an item, or an order, and changes in that definition between years can manufacture an apparent efficiency improvement without any real waste reduction.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_350"
  },
  {
    "term": "landfill-diversion rate inclusion of waste-to-energy",
    "why_it_matters": "Diversion rates commonly count incineration with energy recovery as 'diverted,' masking whether material is actually recycled or merely combusted, which carries very different environmental implications.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_351"
  },
  {
    "term": "operational boundary for waste accounting (owned vs. third-party facilities)",
    "why_it_matters": "Recycling and composting rates may cover only Amazon-operated fulfillment centers and exclude third-party logistics partners, marketplace seller operations, or customer packaging, understating true waste generation.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_352"
  },
  {
    "term": "organic certification standard and chain-of-custody scope",
    "why_it_matters": "Aggregating 'organic' across varying national or private certification schemes with different input restrictions and audit rigor can inflate the apparent environmental benefit of the organic share figure.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_367"
  },
  {
    "term": "product-category cherry-picking (fresh produce vs. all products)",
    "why_it_matters": "Reporting a high organic share for fresh produce separately from all products selects the category most favorable to organic penetration, giving a misleading impression of overall organic commitment.",
    "example_claim_id": "amzn_sr_368"
  }
]
